<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-language-structures" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Language Structures &amp; English Grammar</title>
    <introduction>

      <p>
        As with the other the sections of the Knowledge Structures chapter, the priority is not exhaustive documentation of
        best practices, common conventions, or rules of thumb. In particular, the Language Structures &amp; English Grammar section is
        meant to be a practical learning interface specifically tailored to the accommodate the author's life-long procrastination of taking on the responsibility caring about grammar :logo:.
        The current end-goal is to have an official reference hub for the author to do some much needed 'cognitive upgrading' in basic grammatical proficiency.
        That way she can better communicate ideas that decreases her reliance on other humans and/or AI to do the grammar dirty work for her and gives her independence as a writer.
      </p>

    </introduction>



</section>